  <details class="dict-tile" id="chapter-movement-basics">
    <summary class="dict-tile-summary">
      <h2 class="dict-tile-title">Movement Basics</h2>
      <span class="dict-tile-hint">Twelve foundational moves and how they relate.</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="dict-tile-body">
  <section class="content-section" id="section-core-concepts">
    <p>
      Freestyle has its own words for the moves: the surfaces you catch the
      bag on, the leg circles and body turns that shape a trick, and the
      families tricks grow into. Small movements combine into named tricks,
      and every named trick is in the
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks">trick dictionary</a>.
    </p>

    {{!-- Single-authority rule: operator / modifier ADD values, structure, and X-Dex
          behavior are canonical in src/content/freestyleOperatorReference.ts. Any
          operator/modifier wording on this page (this section, the X-Dex term, the
          modifier feel cards) is secondary and must stay consistent with it; edit
          operatorReference first, then reconcile here. --}}
    <h3 class="section-heading" id="tricks-sets-modifiers">Tricks, sets, and modifiers</h3>
    <p>
      Three kinds of named thing make up the vocabulary. They are related but
      not interchangeable.
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt>Trick</dt>
      <dd>
        A recognized movement pattern that resolves to a defined outcome, usually
        a stall or kick (Mirage, Butterfly, Osis, Torque). A small number resolve
        through a swing element instead (Pendulum, Rake). Tricks are catalogued in
        the <a href="/freestyle/tricks">trick dictionary</a>.
      </dd>
      <dt>Set</dt>
      <dd>
        An entry mechanism that launches into another movement (Pixie, Fairy,
        Stepping, Atomic, Quantum). A set initiates movement rather than ending
        it, so it becomes a trick only when combined with a terminal outcome such
        as a stall or kick (Pixie Set &rarr; Pixie, Fairy Set &rarr; Fairy). That
        is why one word can name both a set and a trick. Sets have their own
        <a href="/freestyle/sets">Set Encyclopedia</a>.
      </dd>
      <dt>Modifier</dt>
      <dd>
        A transformation applied to an existing trick, not normally performed on
        its own (Spinning, Ducking, Symposium, Paradox). The one-line
        tell: <strong>Spinning is a modifier; Spin is a trick.</strong> Modifiers
        live on <a href="/freestyle/operators">Operators &amp; Modifiers</a> and in
        the <a href="#section-modifiers">modifiers section</a> below.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      The <a href="/freestyle/glossary">Glossary</a> gives each term a short
      definition; these chapters explain how the terms fit together.
    </p>

    {{!-- ── The twelve foundational moves ─────────────────────────────────
         Each card shows the move's name, ADD and a plain description of the
         movement; deeper teaching sits in titled collapsible panels, each
         lesson taught once. The core-trick-atoms and atom-{slug} ids are kept
         for inbound deep links. --}}
    {{#if content.coreAtomEducationalCards.length}}
