  <details class="dict-tile" id="chapter-contact-surfaces">
    <summary class="dict-tile-summary">
      <h2 class="dict-tile-title">Contact Surfaces &amp; Stalls</h2>
      <span class="dict-tile-hint">The surfaces a trick lands on and how each one controls the bag.</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="dict-tile-body">
  <section class="content-section" id="section-surfaces">
    <p>
      A <strong>contact surface</strong> is where the foot or body meets the
      bag. A <strong>stall</strong> holds the bag
      on a surface; a <strong>kick</strong> redirects it. Most stalls are
      1 ADD.
    </p>
    <p>
      Surfaces play two roles. <strong>Foundational surfaces</strong> (toe and
      clipper) are where <em>most</em> tricks end: central to notation, but
      <strong>not browse families</strong>, because grouping by &ldquo;everything
      that ends on clipper&rdquo; would bury the whole dictionary together. Every
      other surface is an <strong>alternative surface</strong>: an unusual catch
      surface, a different <em>axis</em> from families. The platform separates
      <strong>movement structure</strong> (the mechanic a lineage conserves) from
      <strong>contact-surface variation</strong> (where the bag is caught), which
      older vocabularies often blurred: whirl, osis, mirage, butterfly, and
      legover are lineages defined by their mechanic, while sole, cloud, knee,
      cross-body sole, and the head, neck, shoulder and flying-surface variants
      are surface groupings, not canonical families.
    </p>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Foundational surfaces</h3>
    <p>The toe and the clipper anchor most named tricks. The clipper is a cross-body surface:</p>
    <div class="glossary-concept" id="concept-cross-body">
      <p>{{content.crossBodyConcept.line}}</p>
      <details class="glossary-core-atom-collapsible">
        <summary>How it relates</summary>
        <p>{{content.crossBodyConcept.relates}}</p>
      </details>
    </div>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-toe">Toe</dt>
      <dd>Top of the shoe, near the toe-box. The default surface for sets and many stalls. Often abbreviated <code>TOE</code> in operational notation.</dd>
      <dt id="term-clipper">Clipper</dt>
      <dd>Clipper is a cross-body contact surface. The canonical Clipper Stall reads <code>SET &gt; OP CLIP [XBD] [DEL]</code>; its SAME/OP relation is component-relative and is not fixed merely by the contact surface. Often abbreviated <code>CLIP</code>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="glossary-note">
      <strong>&ldquo;Clipper&rdquo; names several things.</strong> The
      <strong>clipper stall</strong> is the inside cross-body catch surface above.
      The <strong>clipper</strong> base trick kicks into that position.
      <strong>Flying clipper</strong> is a flying (no-stall) clipper kick, and
      <strong>toe clipper</strong> and <strong>knee clipper</strong> borrow its
      shape (see Implied contacts below).
    </p>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Foot surfaces</h3>
    <p>Less common as catch surfaces; appear regularly in specialized stalls and named compounds.</p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-inside">Inside</dt>
      <dd>The inner side of the foot. Used in inside stalls and as a transitional contact in compounds that travel across the body.</dd>
      <dt id="term-outside">Outside</dt>
      <dd>The outer side of the foot. Used in outside stalls; pairs with inside in same-foot transitions.</dd>
      <dt id="term-sole">Sole</dt>
      <dd>Bottom of the shoe. Stylistic and specialized; the surface for sole stalls and a number of cross-body variants.</dd>
      <dt id="term-heel">Heel</dt>
      <dd>Back of the shoe. Rare as a catch surface; appears in stylistic stalls and certain trick variants.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Body-region surfaces</h3>
    <p>Stylistic and specialized surfaces; most appear in named compounds rather than as default contact points.</p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-knee">Knee</dt>
      <dd>Top of the knee. Used in knee stalls and knee-balanced positions during routines.</dd>
      <dt id="term-shin">Shin</dt>
      <dd>Front of the lower leg. Surface for shin stalls, which the dictionary names <a href="#term-cloud">cloud stalls</a>.</dd>
      <dt id="term-neck">Neck</dt>
      <dd>Back-of-neck stall. Multiple variants distinguished by which side the bag enters and how the head tilts; most appear in routine choreography.</dd>
      <dt id="term-shoulder">Shoulder</dt>
      <dd>Top of the shoulder. Stylistic; appears in routine work and stall-chaining sequences.</dd>
      <dt id="term-chest">Chest</dt>
      <dd>Upper-chest stall. Stylistic and routine-oriented.</dd>
      <dt id="term-back">Back</dt>
      <dd>Upper-back stall. Specialized and rare; appears in advanced freestyle routines.</dd>
      <dt id="term-head">Head</dt>
      <dd>Top-of-head stall. Stylistic; appears in head-balance sequences and routine work.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Specialized variants &amp; alternative names</h3>
    <p>Named variants for specific surface-and-position combinations that the community treats as their own thing.</p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-flapper">Flapper</dt>
      <dd>A cross-body sole stall (<code>XBD</code> + sole). The community name for the cross-body variant of a sole stall.</dd>
      <dt id="term-cloud">Cloud (cloud stall)</dt>
      <dd>The dictionary's name for a shin stall: &ldquo;cloud&rdquo; is the surface label on trick pages, and &ldquo;shin stall&rdquo; the descriptive form.</dd>
    </dl>
    <h3 class="section-heading" id="implicit-contacts">Implied contacts</h3>
    <p>
      Names routinely leave out a terminal contact the community treats as
      obvious, and the convention is not symmetric:
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt>spin</dt>
      <dd>means a spinning <strong>kick</strong>: the kick is implied; &ldquo;spin&rdquo; names the rotation and the contact is understood.</dd>
      <dt>spinning</dt>
      <dd>by itself is <strong>not a trick</strong>: a modifier with no terminal contact. A trick needs an ending; &ldquo;spinning&rdquo; needs a base to spin.</dd>
      <dt>clipper</dt>
      <dd>means a clipper <strong>stall</strong>: a bare surface name defaults to a stall, which is why kicks name themselves.</dd>
      <dt>flying clipper</dt>
      <dd>conventionally means a flying clipper <strong>kick</strong> (the opposite default), because the name stayed associated with a kicking motion; <strong>flying clipper stall</strong> says &ldquo;stall&rdquo; to contrast it.</dd>
      <dt>toe clipper and knee clipper</dt>
      <dd>are flying-clipper motions with a <strong>contact substitution</strong>: the cross-body shape tucked behind the support leg stays, and only the contact (toe or knee) changes. That makes them <em>surface variations</em>, not separate movement lineages.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      The rule: a bare surface defaults to a <strong>stall</strong>; a motion
      word (spin, spinning) implies a <strong>kick</strong> or needs a base; and
      a name spells out the contact only when the default reading would mislead.
    </p>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      <strong>Swing endings.</strong> Pendulum and Rake are the exception to
      ending on a contact: a controlled swing completes the trick. Swing is a
      movement element, not a contact surface; see
      <a href="#edge-cases-special-structures">Edge cases and special structures</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      <strong>Thigh catch.</strong> A thigh catch traps the bag between the
      thighs, an unusual landing surface rather than a foot surface. It is the
      thigh-surface sibling of the squeeze (also called the pincher stall), which
      traps the bag in the crook of the leg; both score as unusual-surface stalls,
      written <code>THIGH [UNS] [DEL]</code> and <code>[UNS] [DEL]</code>
      (2 ADD each).
    </p>
  </section>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="dict-tile" id="chapter-dexterities">
    <summary class="dict-tile-summary">
      <h2 class="dict-tile-title">Dexterities</h2>
      <span class="dict-tile-hint">The leg motions that shape the middle of a trick.</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="dict-tile-body">
  <section class="content-section" id="section-dexterities">
    <p>
      A <strong>dex</strong> (dexterity) is a motion where the foot circles
      around the bag. The dex is the heart of freestyle; almost every
      named compound trick contains at least one.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Mirage uses one dex.</li>
      <li>Double Around the World uses two dexes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="section-heading">The eight closest relatives</h3>
    <p>
      These eight tricks are usually learned one at a time, but they are
      variations of one movement pattern: a single dex shaped by three choices,
      the direction the leg circles, the side it circles on, and the side the bag
      comes down on. Each is one movement change away from three of the others,
      as the figure below shows.
    </p>
    <figure class="glossary-neighbor-figure">
      <figcaption class="glossary-neighbor-figure-caption">
        Start with any of the eight. Change just one of those three choices, and
        you land on another of the eight.
      </figcaption>
      <ul class="glossary-neighbor-grid">
        {{#each content.movementNeighbors.cards}}
        <li class="glossary-neighbor-card">
          <a class="glossary-neighbor-card-trick" href="{{href}}">{{name}}</a>
          <ul class="glossary-neighbor-moves">
            {{#each moves}}
            <li class="glossary-neighbor-move">
              <span class="glossary-neighbor-change">{{changeLabel}}</span>
              <a class="glossary-neighbor-dest" href="{{toHref}}">{{toName}}</a>
            </li>
            {{/each}}
          </ul>
        </li>
        {{/each}}
      </ul>
    </figure>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Clipper counterparts</h3>
    <p>
      Six of the eight have a closely related clipper counterpart: a
      corresponding movement that finishes on a clipper instead of a toe. If
      you know the toe movement, its counterpart is already half familiar.
    </p>
    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Clipper counterparts">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Toe movement</th><th>Clipper counterpart</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/mirage">Mirage</a></td><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/whirl">Whirl</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/illusion">Illusion</a></td><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/rev_whirl">Reverse Whirl</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/pickup">Pickup</a></td><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/drifter">Drifter</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/legover">Legover</a></td><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/reverse_drifter">Reverse Drifter</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/around_the_world">Around the World</a></td><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/rev_swirl">Reverse Swirl</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/orbit">Orbit</a></td><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/swirl">Swirl</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Repeating a movement</h3>
    <p>
      Vocabulary also grows by repeating a movement:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/freestyle/tricks/swirl">Swirl</a> &rarr; <a href="/freestyle/tricks/ripstein">Ripstein</a> &rarr; <a href="/freestyle/tricks/triple_swirl">Triple Swirl</a></li>
      <li><a href="/freestyle/tricks/around_the_world">Around the World</a> &rarr; <a href="/freestyle/tricks/double_around_the_world">Double Around the World</a> &rarr; <a href="/freestyle/tricks/triple_around_the_world">Triple Around the World</a></li>
      <li><a href="/freestyle/tricks/orbit">Orbit</a> &rarr; <a href="/freestyle/tricks/double_orbit">Double Orbit</a> &rarr; <a href="/freestyle/tricks/triple_orbit">Triple Orbit</a></li>
    </ul>

    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-dexless">Dexless</dt>
      <dd>A trick or component that contains no dex. Dexes are so central to freestyle that the absence of one is itself a named category.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Motion style</h3>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-hippy-leggy">Hippy vs leggy</dt>
      <dd>Every dex is one of two motion styles. A <em>hippy</em> dex draws its motion from the hip: the thigh circles the bag. A <em>leggy</em> dex draws its motion from the knee: the calf circles the bag.</dd>

      <dt id="term-full-half-dex">Full vs half dex</dt>
      <dd>A dex's <em>fullness</em> is how far the leg circles the bag. A <em>full</em> dex circles the bag entirely: the leg starts on one side, circles, and returns to the same side. A <em>half</em> dex circles only partway, finishing on the opposite side.</dd>

      <dt id="term-x-dex">X-Dex</dt>
      <dd>An extra dexterity worth +1. It is scored wherever the operational notation carries the <code>[XDEX]</code> marker on a dex; the notation is the source of truth. For example, the <a href="/freestyle/tricks/atom_smasher">Atom Smasher</a> carries <code>[XDEX]</code> and scores the extra +1, while <a href="/freestyle/tricks/atomic_miraging_butterfly">Atomic Miraging Butterfly</a> does not. X-Dex is its own component: never part of a modifier's own weight, and never assumed from an operator name. (Historically, <code>[XDEX]</code> was recorded on the far form of certain bases such as mirage, illusion, whirl, torque, and drifter; the notation now carries that judgement directly.)</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Direction</h3>
    <div class="glossary-concept" id="concept-direction">
      <p>{{content.directionConcept.line}}</p>
      <details class="glossary-core-atom-collapsible">
        <summary>How it relates</summary>
        <p>{{content.directionConcept.relates}}</p>
      </details>
    </div>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-in-dex">In-dex</dt>
      <dd>A dex that travels <em>inward</em>: the bag arcs toward the body's center line. Paired with in-direction set primitives (pixie, quantum) and visible in compounds like <a href="/freestyle/tricks/dimwalk">Dimwalk</a> (pixie butterfly) and Toe Blur (<a href="/freestyle/tricks/quantum_mirage">quantum mirage</a>).</dd>

      <dt id="term-out-dex">Out-dex</dt>
      <dd>A dex that travels <em>outward</em>: the bag arcs away from the body's center line. Paired with out-direction set primitives (atomic, fairy) and visible in compounds like Atom Smasher (atomic mirage).</dd>

      <dt id="term-rev-zero">Direction reversal (<code>rev(0)</code>)</dt>
      <dd>The operator that reverses a dex's in&harr;out direction while adding no difficulty. Putting <code>rev(0)</code> in front of a trick names its direction-reversed sibling: <a href="/freestyle/tricks/illusion">illusion</a> is the reverse of <a href="/freestyle/tricks/mirage">mirage</a>, pickup the reverse of legover, and orbit the reverse of around-the-world. A reversal preserves entry, terminal, side configuration, and structural role, and keeps the <em>same</em> ADD when direction is the only changed dimension; it never moves a trick between the set and standalone roles. The same direction reversal relates the standalone movement names: illusioning is reversed miraging, exactly as the illusion trick is the reverse of mirage. Hippy and leggy execution notes are stylistic; a reversed pair differs only in dex direction once those are set aside.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Relative-side relationships</h3>
    <div class="glossary-concept" id="concept-side">
      <p>{{content.sideConcept.line}}</p>
      <details class="glossary-core-atom-collapsible">
        <summary>How it relates</summary>
        <p>{{content.sideConcept.relates}}</p>
      </details>
      {{#if content.sideConcept.hasReveal}}
      <details class="glossary-core-atom-collapsible">
        <summary>What it reveals</summary>
        <p>{{content.sideConcept.reveal}}</p>
      </details>
      {{/if}}
    </div>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-same-side"><code>SAME</code> (same-side)</dt>
      <dd>The dex acts on the same leg as the most recent side-bearing component (abbreviated <code>ss</code>). It is not implied by a clipper or toe entry.</dd>

      <dt id="term-opposite-side"><code>OP</code> (opposite)</dt>
      <dd>The dex acts on the opposite leg from the most recent side-bearing component (abbreviated <code>op</code>). <code>SAME</code> and <code>OP</code> are different executions even when they score the same.</dd>

      <dt id="term-paradox-side">Paradox (<code>PDX</code>)</dt>
      <dd>Paradox marks a distinct relationship on a dexterity. It is not a third SAME/OP value; <code>[PDX]</code> can coexist with <code>SAME</code> or <code>OP</code>, and independently with <code>[XDEX]</code>.</dd>

      <dt id="term-op-not-xdex"><code>OP</code> is not X-Dex</dt>
      <dd><code>OP</code> is a component-relative leg relation: the dex acts on the opposite leg from the most recent side-bearing component. Near and far are other names for these relations: near is <code>SAME</code>, far is <code>OP</code>. Neither adds difficulty on its own. <a href="#term-x-dex">X-Dex</a> is a separate +1, scored only where the operational notation carries <code>[XDEX]</code>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Execution window</h3>
    <p>The terms below locate a dex within the space the leg draws around the bag.</p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-dex-window">Dex window</dt>
      <dd>The phantom space the leg draws around the bag while the dex is performed.</dd>

      <dt id="term-deep">Deep</dt>
      <dd>A dex that sits high in the window, well away from the shoe. Controlled and clearly executed; the bag travels through a generous arc around the leg.</dd>

      <dt id="term-thin">Thin</dt>
      <dd>A dex that sits at the edge of the window, close to the shoe. The bag barely clears the leg; legal and counted, but the safety margin is small.</dd>

      <dt id="term-shoey">Shoey</dt>
      <dd>A dex that grazes the shoe surface during the circle. Distinct from a clean thin dex by the audible / visible shoe contact.</dd>

      <dt id="term-the">The</dt>
      <dd>Pronounced &ldquo;thuh,&rdquo; not &ldquo;thee.&rdquo; A component that is attempted but completely missed: a dex that misses the window entirely, or a duck done completely above the head. Typically counted as a fault.</dd>

      <dt id="term-scoopy">Scoopy</dt>
      <dd>A dex with extra under-bag character, where the leg cups beneath the bag during the circle rather than circling cleanly around it.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Execution quality</h3>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-clean">Clean</dt>
      <dd>Fully controlled, clearly executed. The reference quality every other label is measured against.</dd>

      <dt id="term-sloppy">Sloppy</dt>
      <dd>Poorly controlled execution. The dex completes but the motion is unclear or the bag's path is uneven.</dd>

      <dt id="term-pulled">Pulled</dt>
      <dd>A set where the bag is dragged through an uptime dex or a spin before the intended component happens, for example a stepping set where the leg passes over the bag while it stays on the set foot. Used interchangeably with <a href="#term-slurry">slurry</a>.</dd>

      <dt id="term-slurry">Slurry</dt>
      <dd>Another name for a <a href="#term-pulled">pulled</a> set.</dd>

      <dt id="term-froggy">Froggy</dt>
      <dd>A pulled spin: a spin that drags the bag through before the intended component happens.</dd>

      <dt id="term-over-dexed">Over-dexed</dt>
      <dd>A dex with too much motion for what the trick requires: extra leg travel that doesn't serve the structure. Common in early learners working a new dex pattern.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Ducks</h3>
    <p>A <strong>duck</strong> dips the head so the bag passes around the neck. The four variations differ by head movement and where the bag ends up (the <a href="#section-modifiers">ducking ecosystem</a> covers how they modify a base):</p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-ducking"><span id="glossary-panel-ducking"></span>Ducking</dt>
      <dd>Head moves toward the bag; the bag falls to the opposite side from the head.</dd>

      <dt id="term-weaving">Weaving</dt>
      <dd>A ducking set in which the bag is caught on the same foot that performed the set.</dd>

      <dt id="term-diving">Diving</dt>
      <dd>Head moves over the bag and back under to its original position; the bag falls to the same side as the initial head movement.</dd>

      <dt id="term-zulu">Zulu</dt>
      <dd>A ducking set in which the bag travels across the body, under the chin, before the duck.</dd>

      <dt id="term-crowny">Crowny</dt>
      <dd>A duck-quality term: a duck performed with just the very top of the head, the duck-world parallel to a thin or <a href="#term-shoey">shoey</a> dex.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="dict-tile" id="chapter-timing-sets">
    <summary class="dict-tile-summary">
      <h2 class="dict-tile-title">Timing &amp; Sets</h2>
      <span class="dict-tile-hint">Where each move happens during a trick, and the sets that begin it.</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="dict-tile-body">
  <section class="content-section" id="section-timing-sets">
    <p>
      A trick has a clock. The <strong>set</strong> positions the bag; the
      bag rises, peaks, falls; the trick catches. Modifiers (see the
      Operators &amp; Modifiers section, below) are classified by which
      beat they transform.
    </p>

    {{! ── Timing-clock visual ──────────────────────────────────────────
        Horizontal "trick clock": the bag's parabolic arc maps onto the
        four-beat structure. Inline SVG; no client JS. Its colours come from
        the stylesheet's tokens, so the drawing follows the site palette. }}
    <figure class="glossary-timing-clock-figure" aria-labelledby="timing-clock-caption">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 600 200" role="img" aria-label="A horizontal timeline showing the four-beat structure of a freestyle trick: set, uptime, midtime (peak), downtime, catch. The bag's path is drawn as a rising-and-falling arc." class="glossary-timing-clock">
        <line x1="50" y1="160" x2="550" y2="160" stroke-width="1.25" />
        <path d="M 80 160 Q 300 30, 520 160" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <circle cx="80"  cy="160" r="5" />
        <circle cx="190" cy="95"  r="5" />
        <circle cx="300" cy="50"  r="6" />
        <circle cx="410" cy="95"  r="5" />
        <circle cx="520" cy="160" r="5" />
        <g font-family="inherit" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">
          <text x="80"  y="183">Set</text>
          <text x="190" y="118">Uptime</text>
          <text x="300" y="33"  font-weight="600">Midtime</text>
          <text x="410" y="118">Downtime</text>
          <text x="520" y="183">Catch</text>
        </g>
        <g font-family="inherit" font-size="11" font-style="italic" text-anchor="middle">
          <text x="190" y="135">bag rising</text>
          <text x="300" y="68">peak</text>
          <text x="410" y="135">bag falling</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="glossary-timing-clock-caption" id="timing-clock-caption">
        The four-beat clock: a trick begins with a set, the bag rises through uptime, peaks at midtime, falls through downtime, and ends in a catch (stall or kick).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-set">Set</dt>
      <dd>The launch action that begins the trick and positions the bag. A dex performed as part of that launch has a set role; the same movement performed after the launch, before the final catch, is a standalone realization. Which role a trick uses follows its established structure and execution, not a single timing instant.</dd>
      <dt id="term-set-realization">Set realization</dt>
      <dd>A dex performed as part of launching and positioning the bag. It can terminate immediately on a toe and still be a genuine set; a following component makes the role easier to recognize but is not required. Realized as sets, the inward and outward movements are named Quantum and Atomic.</dd>
      <dt id="term-standalone-realization">Standalone realization</dt>
      <dd>A dex performed on its own once the bag is launched, before the final catch, rather than as part of the launch: the inward and outward standalone tricks are Mirage and Illusion. Miraging and Illusioning describe those movements in established names such as Miraging Kick and Illusioning Kick, but they are not reusable scored operators and do not license arbitrary compounds.</dd>
      <dt id="term-uptime">Uptime</dt>
      <dd>Before the peak. The bag is rising.</dd>
      <dt id="term-midtime">Midtime (hangtime)</dt>
      <dd>At the peak. The brief no-plant moment.</dd>
      <dt id="term-downtime">Downtime</dt>
      <dd>After the peak. The bag is falling toward the catch.</dd>
      <dt id="term-phases-sides">Phases and sides</dt>
      <dd>Tricks break into the three phases above by bag direction. A <strong>side modifier</strong>, <code>ss</code> or <code>op</code>, connects each component to its predecessor (see <a href="#term-same-side">relative-side relationships</a>).</dd>
      <dt id="term-attack">Attack</dt>
      <dd>How quickly a player begins the next phase's component. A transition that has to fire aggressively (like attacking the mirage quickly after the stepping set in Blur) is said to need a fast attack.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">How tricks begin</h3>
    <p>
      Two set surfaces dominate how tricks begin: toe and clipper.
      The launch sets Pixie, Fairy, and Stepping recur widely, with operators
      such as Paradox and Symposium layered alongside them. The set surface
      itself is too broad to be a system.
    </p>

    <h3 class="section-heading" id="set-surface-vs-named-set">Set surface vs named set</h3>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-toe-set">Toe set</dt>
      <dd>A set launched from a toe stall.</dd>
      <dt id="term-clip-set">Clip set (clipper set)</dt>
      <dd>A set launched from a clipper stall.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Three ideas sit close together and are easy to confuse. A <strong>set
      surface</strong> (toe or clip) is simply where the set begins. A
      <strong>named set</strong> (pixie, atomic, and the others below) is a
      specific launch system layered on that surface. <strong>Execution
      timing</strong> (uptime, midtime, downtime) describes when a motion
      happens. On its own, timing does not decide whether a dex has a set role
      or a standalone role; that follows the trick's established structure and
      execution.
    </p>

    {{! Named-set vocabulary. This is the home for the confirmed set terms, not
        Operators & Modifiers. The confirmed sets mirror the Set Encyclopedia;
        the not-set timing terms carry stable anchors so old /freestyle/sets/:slug
        routes for non-set concepts redirect here. }}
    <h3 class="section-heading">The named sets</h3>
    <p>
      A set opens the movement before the base runs. These sets are named
      vocabulary in their own right and are defined here; some also broadcast
      across other bases as set modifiers.
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-set-launches"><span id="glossary-panel-pixie"></span>Pixie, fairy, stepping</dt>
      <dd>The core launch sets. Pixie and fairy are toe-anchored; stepping is clipper-anchored. Pixie is the Around-the-World movement and Fairy the Orbit movement, each realized with a set role: the same directional shape, with the dex built into the bag's launch.</dd>
      <dt id="term-set-atomic">Atomic</dt>
      <dd>The outward dex realized as a set, contributing +1; any X-Dex is a separate event, not part of atomic. Hippy Atomic and leggy Atomic are execution styles of Atomic, not separate tricks.</dd>
      <dt id="term-set-quantum">Quantum</dt>
      <dd>The inward dex realized as a set; the direction-reversed sibling of Atomic on the inward side.</dd>
      <dt id="term-set-furious">Furious</dt>
      <dd>A two-dex set beginning from clipper with an opposite-side inward dex followed by a same-side inward dex. Barraging is its legacy name (see below).</dd>
      <dt id="term-set-blurry">Blurry</dt>
      <dd>A set modifier that adds one dex as part of the set. Some named blurry compounds, such as Blur, expand to a deeper decomposition rather than a flat blurry-plus-base sum.</dd>
      <dt id="term-set-nuclear">Nuclear</dt>
      <dd>A two-dex set (2 ADD): a paradox dex followed by a downtime illusioning dex.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Related terms that are not launch sets</h3>
    <p>
      A few terms sit near the set vocabulary but are <strong>not</strong> launch
      sets: they are standalone realizations or legacy names.
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-illusioning">Illusioning</dt>
      <dd>A descriptive name for the outward standalone dex movement, as preserved in Illusioning Kick. Atomic is the same movement with a set role; Illusioning is not a reusable scored operator.</dd>
      <dt id="term-miraging-not-a-set">Miraging</dt>
      <dd>A descriptive name for the inward standalone dex movement, as preserved in Miraging Kick. Quantum is the same movement with a set role; Miraging is not a launch set or a reusable scored operator.</dd>
      <dt id="term-barraging-not-a-set">Barraging</dt>
      <dd>A legacy name pattern for the Furious two-dex set, not a separate set or operator; barraging-named tricks resolve to Furious, and their rows and aliases are kept for name continuity.</dd>
      <dt id="term-barrage-not-a-set">Barrage</dt>
      <dd>A separate standalone base trick concept, not the same as Barraging.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="detail-muted" id="term-set-source-language">
      Some sources describe sets through familiar downtime motions (illusioning,
      miraging, or barraging language) because those shapes are easy to picture.
      The encyclopedia keeps that language as teaching shorthand, and many are
      the names tricks are still known by, but the set taxonomy uses the modern
      set names: a descriptive reading is not automatically an equivalent set
      name.
    </p>
  </section>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="dict-tile" id="chapter-operators-modifiers">
    <summary class="dict-tile-summary">
      <h2 class="dict-tile-title">Operators &amp; Modifiers</h2>
      <span class="dict-tile-hint">The transformations that turn a base trick into new tricks.</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="dict-tile-body">
  <section class="content-section" id="section-modifiers">
    {{! The standalone link navigates away, so it stays in the body: the cards
        in the grid above all open in place, and a control that leaves the page
        must not sit among them. }}
    <p><a class="glossary-section-canonical-link" href="/freestyle/operators">Open standalone</a></p>

    {{! The set-vs-standalone distinction is explained in full under Timing & Sets
        (set and standalone realization); this card keeps its line and the
        reveal, which is the insight that card adds. }}
    <div class="glossary-concept" id="concept-set-vs-operator">
      <p>{{content.setVsOperatorConcept.line}}</p>
      {{#if content.setVsOperatorConcept.hasReveal}}
      <details class="glossary-core-atom-collapsible">
        <summary>What it reveals</summary>
        <p>{{content.setVsOperatorConcept.reveal}}</p>
      </details>
      {{/if}}
    </div>
    <div class="glossary-concept" id="concept-composition">
      <p>{{content.compositionConcept.line}}</p>
      <details class="glossary-core-atom-collapsible">
        <summary>How it relates</summary>
        <p>{{content.compositionConcept.relates}}</p>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="glossary-note" id="paradox-symposium-compare">
      <h3>Paradox and Symposium, side by side</h3>
      <p>
        Two operators new players often confuse, because both change how the
        body carries a dex rather than adding one.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Paradox</strong> changes the body-and-support relationship
          around one dex: the hips pivot and the body switches sides. It scores
          +1, and <code>[PDX]</code> marks the affected dex. It adds no dex, and
          it is not simply a far or cross-body dex.</li>
        <li><strong>Symposium</strong> imposes a no-replant discipline: the
          support leg stays off the ground through the dex. It scores +1.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        They stack, as in paradox symposium whirl, because they constrain
        different aspects of the same dex: paradox the side the body works on,
        symposium the support leg.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Modifiers form <strong>ecosystems</strong>. A single modifier transforms
      <em>many</em> different base tricks: body and rotational ecosystems
      (ducking, gyro, spinning) reshape the midtime. The named sets are defined
      in <a href="#section-timing-sets">Timing &amp; Sets</a>; several also
      broadcast across other bases as set modifiers, which is why pixie appears
      across pixie-illusion, pixie-mobius, and symposium-pixie rather than as a
      family of its own. An ecosystem is
      <strong>not a family</strong>: it has no terminal mechanic of its own; it
      modifies a base that does. Browse a launch-set ecosystem under
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks?view=set">By set</a> and a body or timing
      ecosystem under <a href="/freestyle/tricks?view=modifier">By modifier</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 class="section-heading" id="lineage-or-ecosystem">Lineage or ecosystem? A test you can apply</h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>lineage</strong> (a <a href="#section-families">family</a> or a
      descendant lineage) has an ending of its own; an <strong>ecosystem</strong>
      is a transformation you apply to someone else&rsquo;s ending. One quick
      test settles most cases: <em>can you do it on its own?</em> You can do
      &ldquo;a whirl&rdquo; or &ldquo;an osis&rdquo;; they land. You cannot
      do &ldquo;a ducking,&rdquo; only a <em>ducking-something</em> (ducking-osis,
      ducking-mirage, ducking-clipper).
    </p>
    <p>
      The set primitives are the edge case. <strong>Pixie</strong> and
      <strong>fairy</strong> broadcast across other bases like an ecosystem
      (pixie-illusion, symposium-pixie), yet each also lands as its own trick
      when taken to a toe stall: &ldquo;a pixie&rdquo; is the set to an
      opposite-toe stall, a real 2-ADD trick. They read as both, depending on
      how they are used.
    </p>
    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="lineage-or-ecosystem">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Productive lineage</th><th>Modifier ecosystem</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Own terminal mechanic</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What it contributes</td><td>a base trick to build on</td><td>a transformation of a base</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Spread</td><td>a coherent family tree</td><td>broadcast across many unrelated bases</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Example</td><td>whirl, osis</td><td>ducking, spinning, blurry</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      <strong>Hard cases: symposium and paradox.</strong> Older usage sometimes
      spoke of them as families, but both transform many bases and have no
      terminal mechanic of their own, so the platform classifies them as
      ecosystems: by the test above, neither lands on its own.
    </p>

    {{> freestyle-modifier-reference}}
  </section>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="dict-tile" id="chapter-family-encyclopedia">
    <summary class="dict-tile-summary">
      <h2 class="dict-tile-title">Family Encyclopedia</h2>
      <span class="dict-tile-hint">How tricks organize into lineages.</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="dict-tile-body">
  <section class="content-section" id="section-families">
    <p>
      A <strong>family</strong> is a <strong>productive movement lineage</strong>:
      tricks that share a conserved <strong>terminal mechanic</strong>,
      decompose into the same skeleton, and carry a substantial set of named
      descendants the community reasons about, not merely &ldquo;any trick that
      has spin-offs.&rdquo; The terminal mechanic is <em>inherited</em>: every
      descendant still ends the family&rsquo;s way (paradox-whirl is still a
      whirl), while the modifiers stacked on the way in are <em>used</em>, not
      inherited as identity.
    </p>

    <h3 class="section-heading">What makes a family?</h3>
    <p>
      Whirl is the model: every whirl ends <em>leggy-in dexterity into a
      cross-body clipper</em>, and dozens of tricks are built on that ending. Two
      things look like families but are not:
      <strong>Foundational Terminal Surfaces</strong> (toe, clipper), too broad to group by (see
      <a href="#section-surfaces">contact surfaces</a>), and
      <strong>modifier ecosystems</strong> (ducking, spinning), which have no
      ending of their own (see <a href="#section-modifiers">modifiers</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      Several kinds of object get loosely called &ldquo;families,&rdquo; and
      keeping them apart is the key to reading the dictionary:
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-compositional-premise-examples">
      <dt>Family</dt>
      <dd>A <strong>conserved-terminal lineage</strong>: a structure tricks
        land into, whose signature descendants keep even as modifiers stack
        (paradox-whirl still <em>is</em> a whirl). Every family is read on two
        independent axes. Its <strong>lineage position</strong> is either a
        <em>root</em> (Mirage, Whirl, Swirl) or a <em>branch</em> of another family
        (Torque and Blender branch from Osis). A <strong>Family Parent</strong>
        has a page of its own; a <strong>Minor Lineage</strong> is listed in the
        family view without one. A branch can be a Family Parent (Torque), and a
        root can be a Minor Lineage (Eclipse). The family cards below show
        both.</dd>
      <dt>Atom / primitive</dt>
      <dd>A foundational building block performed <em>within</em> tricks (an
        entry, orbit, or swing move), never a terminal lineage.
        Around-the-world and orbit are the clearest cases.</dd>
      <dt>Modifier ecosystem</dt>
      <dd>Transforms many bases and has no terminal of its own: both
        <em>sets</em> (pixie, fairy) and <em>body modifiers</em> (ducking, spinning).
        See <a href="#section-modifiers">modifiers</a>.</dd>
      <dt>Family anchor</dt>
      <dd>A <strong>family-anchor trick</strong> is the canonical trick that acts
        as a family's productive root, with its own trick-detail page and its
        group in the
        <a href="/freestyle/tricks?view=family">By family</a> browse, which
        renders the same roster as this chapter. Not every trick is an anchor
        (flurry, witchdoctor, and paradox-mirage are tricks, not anchors); every
        trick, in or out of a family, stays findable in the
        <a href="/freestyle/tricks">main dictionary</a> and search.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="glossary-note">
      A <strong>Family Parent</strong> conserves a recognizable terminal identity
      that descendants inherit and appears on an explicit curated first-class
      roster. First-class status is an editorial classification, not a trick
      count. Conserved-terminal lineages not on that roster are
      <strong>Minor Lineages</strong>; some legacy &ldquo;families&rdquo; are really
      <a href="#section-modifiers">modifier ecosystems</a>, surfaces, or aliases.
    </p>

    <p class="glossary-note">
      <strong>Where the edges blur.</strong> Some groupings are pragmatic, not
      provable. The rotational cluster <strong>whirl / swirl / twirl</strong> is
      grouped together because the community reasons about them together, not
      because there is one airtight mechanic. <strong>illusion / pickup /
      legover</strong> share a leggy-over feel that resists a single boundary;
      they stay three separate parents.
      <strong>eclipse / butterfly-swirl</strong> and the <strong>drifter</strong>
      lineage are historical and cultural clusters as much as structural ones.
      Some of the browse's lines are drawn by usage and judgement, and a future
      ruling may move them.
    </p>

    <h3 class="section-heading" id="edge-cases-special-structures">Edge cases and special structures</h3>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      A few recognized structures sit outside the standard model (a trick resolves
      to a stall or kick; a family conserves a terminal identity). They are gathered
      here so they read as deliberate exceptions, not as gaps.
    </p>

    <p class="glossary-note">
      <strong>Swing-element tricks.</strong> A small number of recognized tricks
      terminate through a controlled swing element rather than a stall or kick: the
      swing motion itself completes the trick, which may then settle into a stall, a
      kick, a catch, or a follow-on move. Pendulum and Rake are the primary examples
      (the same toe-and-swing pair in reversed order: toe then swing, swing then toe).
      In notation the swing appears as a <code>SWING</code> token, scored as a dex
      only when it carries <code>[DEX]</code> (as in Rake, <code>SET &gt; SWING [DEX]
      &gt; SAME TOE [DEL]</code>); Pendulum's <code>TOE SWING (SET)</code> carries no
      bracket flags, so its difficulty is taken from the trick's recorded ADD rather
      than derived from the notation.
    </p>

    <p class="glossary-note">
      <strong>Swirl behaves differently from most dex families.</strong> A swirl
      resolves naturally to a clipper stall, and unlike most dexterity families it
      has no recognized kick variant. The family grows through structural variants
      (whirling swirl, reverse swirling) rather than kick forms.
    </p>

    <p class="glossary-note">
      <strong>Continuous-Control Lineage</strong> (an observational grouping, not
      a formal family). <a href="/freestyle/tricks/walk_over">Walkover</a>,
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks/wrap">wrap</a>,
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks/hop_over">hopover</a> (which adds an airborne
      crossing) and <a href="/freestyle/tricks/eclipse">eclipse</a> (a
      symposium-style airborne form) all begin from an inside stall and stay
      under continuous control, resolving to a clipper position; they differ by
      the body movement around the support leg (compared in the
      <a href="#inside-clipper-neighborhood">inside-stall case study</a>).
    </p>

    <h3 class="section-heading">Family roster</h3>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      The dictionary&rsquo;s Family Parents, the same set the
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks?view=family">By family</a> browse uses. A Family
      Parent that inherits a parent&rsquo;s terminal identity is shown as a branch
      in parentheses. Excluded: the Foundational Terminal Surfaces (toe-stall,
      clipper-stall), modifier ecosystems (pixie, fairy), and entry primitives
      (around-the-world, orbit).
    </p>
    <p class="detail-muted glossary-family-roster">
      {{#each content.firstClassFamilyRoster}}{{#unless @first}} &middot; {{/unless}}<a href="{{{href}}}">{{label}}</a>{{#if branches.length}} ({{#each branches}}{{#unless @first}}, {{/unless}}<a href="{{{href}}}">{{label}}</a>{{/each}}){{/if}}{{/each}}
    </p>
    <p class="detail-muted glossary-family-roster">
      <strong>Minor lineages</strong> ({{content.minorLineageRoster.length}}), browsable
      in the family view without standalone family pages:
      {{#each content.minorLineageRoster}}{{#unless @first}} &middot; {{/unless}}<a href="{{{href}}}">{{label}}</a>{{/each}}
    </p>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      Each family below has a card, grouped by tier (Family Parents, then Minor
      Lineages) and within each tier by lineage position (roots, then branches
      under their parent).
    </p>
    {{#each content.familyCardGroups}}
    <h3 class="section-heading">{{tierLabel}}</h3>
    {{#each subGroups}}
    <p class="glossary-family-subgroup-label">{{label}}</p>
    <div class="glossary-family-card-grid">
      {{#each cards}}
      {{> glossary-family-card}}
      {{/each}}
    </div>
    {{/each}}
    {{/each}}

    {{! Core atoms registry — preserved for #term-{slug} anchor stability
        on atom slugs that do not map 1:1 to a family card (clipper-stall /
