  <details class="dict-tile" id="chapter-structural-analysis">
    <summary class="dict-tile-summary">
      <h2 class="dict-tile-title">Structural Analysis</h2>
      <span class="dict-tile-hint">How to read any trick name as a formula.</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="dict-tile-body">
  <section class="content-section" id="section-notation">

    {{! No in-section mini-TOC here: the section's anchors are deep-link
        targets inside this collapsed chapter, revealed on arrival by the
        glossary deep-link opener. }}
    <p class="glossary-thesis">
      The language evolves by compressing recurring compositional structures
      into shorter readable symbolic forms.
    </p>

    <p class="glossary-operator-notation-framing">
      Operational notation is the compact symbolic shorthand for trick
      composition. <em>Paradox mirage</em>, for example, reads
      <code class="notation-display-tokens"><span class="notation-token">CLIP</span> &gt; <span class="notation-token">OP IN</span> <span class="notation-token">[PDX]</span> <span class="notation-token">[DEX]</span> &gt; <span class="notation-token">OP TOE</span> <span class="notation-token">[DEL]</span></code>:
      a clipper set, an opposite-leg in-out dex carrying the paradox marker, and
      a toe catch. The notation lets readers reconstruct the body mechanics from
      the name without memorizing each compound. For the full operational
      grammar and worked decompositions see
      <a href="/freestyle/add-analysis">ADD Accounting &amp; Analysis</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="glossary-concept" id="concept-add">
      <p>{{content.addConcept.line}}</p>
      <details class="glossary-core-atom-collapsible">
        <summary>How it relates</summary>
        <p>{{content.addConcept.relates}}</p>
      </details>
      {{#if content.addConcept.hasReveal}}
      <details class="glossary-core-atom-collapsible">
        <summary>What it reveals</summary>
        <p>{{content.addConcept.reveal}}</p>
      </details>
      {{/if}}
    </div>


    {{! ── Compositional premise — Ben Job (1995) ───────────────────────────
        Concise primer naming Ben Job's notation contribution explicitly
        (per the codified notation-tradition exception). Frames the
        premise + 4 examples + forward-link to the Set Encyclopedia's
        how-sets-are-built section for the systematic exploration. The canonical formula itself lives
        in Jobs notation below; this subsection refers there rather
        than duplicating. }}
    <h3 class="section-heading" id="compositional-premise">The compositional premise: every trick as formula (Ben Job, 1995)</h3>
    <p>
      The notation system descends from grammar work originating with
      Ben Job (footbag discussion list, 1995). Job proposed that every
      freestyle trick can be expressed as a small grammar of set-type,
      side, direction, and dexterity tokens. The base shape is set out
      under <a href="#jobs-notation">Jobs notation</a> below.
    </p>
    <p>
      The dictionary's operational notation system descends from this
      compositional grammar, extending it with additional movement
      primitives and modifiers: swirl/twirl, body modifiers
      (ducking, diving, spinning), no-plant variants (symposium), unusual
      surfaces, and torque-class hybrids. The base grammar covers a large
      central region of the trick language; the extensions reach into the
      rest.
    </p>
    <p>
      Within that scope, several named "sets" turn out to be recognizable
      trick structures under a different reading: <em>blurry</em> decomposes
      to stepping with a paradox second dex. The
      <a href="/freestyle/sets#how-sets-are-built">Set Encyclopedia</a> explores
      these compositional relationships systematically.
    </p>
    <p class="glossary-note">
      These are <strong>documented readings</strong>, not all settled doctrine; a
      decomposition records how a name is structurally understood today, which is not the
      same as a canonical ruling. See <a href="#tracking-vs-canonization">Tracking is not
      canonization</a>.
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-compositional-premise-examples">
      <dt>Pixie</dt>    <dd><code>TOE &gt; SAME IN [DEX] &gt;</code></dd>
      <dt>Stepping</dt> <dd><code>CLIP &gt; OP IN [DEX] &gt;</code></dd>
      <dt>Blurry</dt>   <dd><code>CLIP &gt; OP IN [DEX] &gt; OP OUT [DEX] &gt;</code></dd>
      <dt>Mobius</dt>   <dd><code>CLIP &gt;&gt; (back) SPIN [BOD] &gt;&gt; SAME IN [DEX] &gt; (front) SPIN [BOD] &gt; OP CLIP [XBD] [DEL]</code></dd>
    </dl>

    {{! ── Jobs notation (historical source) ────────────────────────────────
        Dedicated reference section for Job's original 1995 notation
        proposal, the gold-standard historical source for the grammar
        lineage. Quoted material below is verbatim from the project
        archive of the article; the rendered paragraph cites the article by
        title, with no internal path on this public surface. Per the codified
        notation-tradition exception, naming Ben Job is permitted here. }}
    <h3 class="section-heading" id="jobs-notation">Jobs notation</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Jobs notation</strong> is the original compositional grammar Ben
      Job proposed. His article, the authoritative historical source for the
      grammar, sets out its core:
    </p>
    <pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="jobs-notation"><code class="font-mono">(toe | clip) &gt;
[(same | op)(in | out) dexterity]* &gt;
(same | op)(toe | clip)</code></pre>
    <p>
      Read informally: a trick starts with a set surface (toe or clip),
      contains zero or more dex events with same/op side and in/out
      direction, and terminates with a catch surface. The original
      article notes that <em>same</em>, <em>op</em>, and <em>no plant</em>
      are all relative to the leg previously referred to.
    </p>
    <p>
      A selection of Job's original named examples:
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Mirage</dt>           <dd><code>toe &gt; op in dex &gt; op toe</code></dd>
      <dt>Eggbeater</dt>        <dd><code>toe &gt; op out dex &gt; op out dex &gt; same toe</code></dd>
      <dt>Symposium Mirage</dt> <dd><code>toe &gt; (no plant while) &gt; op in dex &gt; op toe</code></dd>
      <dt>Symposium Whirl</dt>  <dd><code>toe &gt; (no plant while) &gt; op in dex &gt; op clip</code></dd>
      <dt>Ripwalk</dt>          <dd><code>clip &gt; op in dex &gt; op out dex &gt; op clip</code></dd>
      <dt>Double Pickup</dt>    <dd><code>clip &gt; op in dex &gt; same in dex &gt; same toe</code></dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The article anticipates the modifier extensions later codified by
      this dictionary: Pogo / Symposium / Spinning / Gyro,
      Stomping, Blind, hop-over, Carry (Wrap), duck, dive,
      jumping/flying, Swirl/Twirl cross-body dex, and swing (Pendulum,
      Rake), noting that the set of primitives is finite and the
      space of tricks they generate is effectively complete.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted">
      Source: Ben Job, <em>By the Way, Not the Name</em>, message to the
      footbag.org mailing list, October 31, 1995.
    </p>
    <p>
      The grammar's element order matters: Pendulum (toe then swing) and Rake
      (swing then toe) are two distinct primitives built from the same elements,
      with an identical ADD.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      <a class="action-link" href="/freestyle/notation-article">To See the Entire Article, Press Here.</a>
    </p>

    {{! ── Generative Insight ───────────────────────────────────────────────
        Surfaces the compositional-completeness framing that's implicit in
        the notation but never stated explicitly. Two paragraphs + the
        canonical structural formula. Honors the notation tradition
        without biographical attribution. }}
    <h3 class="section-heading" id="generative-insight">Generative insight</h3>
    <p>
      The notation carries a stronger claim than mere shorthand. A
      small set of primitive movement operators (a starting
      surface, a finite sequence of dex events, a terminating surface)
      provides a generative core for a large part of freestyle trick
      structure, extended by further movement primitives, terminals and
      modifiers. Within that core the tricks are, in principle,
      <strong>enumerable</strong>: pick a starting surface, pick a
      finite sequence of operators, read off the resulting formula.
      The community-named tricks are the formulas the community found
      worth naming. Many unnamed-but-structurally-valid tricks exist;
      naming follows usage, not exhaustive enumeration.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Job's grammar above, the <code>*</code> marks the iterable interior:
      one dex produces a simple compound, two a deeper one, and three or more
      the multi-dex names (<em>blurry</em>, <em>barraging</em>,
      <em>flurry</em>) that compress longer chains under a single folk name.
      The skeleton does not describe every recognised trick: kicks can also
      stand as tricks in their own right. The trick
      dictionary is a <em>curated subset</em> of this formula's
      generative output, not its full closure. Modifiers (paradox,
      symposium, spinning, ducking) modulate the
      <code>(same | op)(in | out)</code> choices and the body
      configuration during the dex events; their effects layer onto
      the same generative skeleton.
    </p>

    {{! ── Glyph quick-reference ──────────────────────────────────────────
        Visible-symbol strip at the top of §7 so the section opens with
        concrete glyphs rather than prose. Data sourced from
        content.operatorBoard.tiers; tier colors match the shared
        operator-board partial's tier palette. }}
    <div class="glossary-glyph-quickref">
      {{#each content.operatorBoard.tiers}}
      <div class="glossary-glyph-quickref-tier glossary-glyph-quickref-tier--{{key}}">
        <span class="glossary-glyph-quickref-tier-label">{{title}}</span>
        <ul class="glossary-glyph-quickref-list">
          {{#each operators}}
          <li class="glossary-glyph-quickref-cell">
            <span class="glossary-glyph-quickref-glyph">{{glyph}}</span>
            <span class="glossary-glyph-quickref-name">{{name}}</span>
          </li>
          {{/each}}
        </ul>
      </div>
      {{/each}}
    </div>

    {{! The three worked examples below (whirl, paradox whirl, gauntlet) are the
        single home for the base / single-modifier / deep-compound progression. }}
    <p>
      The longer you stack operators on a base, the longer the name, until the
      community converges on a shorter shorthand; all forms are legitimate, and
      the community uses the shortest readable one. Mobius is unpacked depth by
      depth in the <a href="#symbolic-compression-flow">symbolic-compression
      flow</a>.
    </p>
    {{! Operator → named-compound block. Five compact compositions, all
        visible-token, demonstrate how operators stacked on bases produce
        the named tricks that appear in the dictionary. }}
    <p>Notation makes composition visible. Operator + base &rarr; named compound:</p>
    <div class="glossary-notation-mappings">
      <p class="glossary-notation-row">
        <code class="notation-display-tokens glossary-inline-notation"><span class="notation-token notation-set">pixie</span> + <span class="notation-token notation-core-family">butterfly</span></code>
        <span class="glossary-notation-shorthand">&rarr; <strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/dimwalk">DIMWALK</a></strong></span>
      </p>
      <p class="glossary-notation-row">
        <code class="notation-display-tokens glossary-inline-notation"><span class="notation-token notation-modifier">stepping</span> + <span class="notation-token notation-core-family">butterfly</span></code>
        <span class="glossary-notation-shorthand">&rarr; <strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/ripwalk">RIPWALK</a></strong></span>
      </p>
      <p class="glossary-notation-row">
        <code class="notation-display-tokens glossary-inline-notation"><span class="notation-token notation-modifier">paradox</span> + <span class="notation-token notation-core-family">whirl</span></code>
        <span class="glossary-notation-shorthand">&rarr; <strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/paradox_whirl">PARADOX WHIRL</a></strong></span>
      </p>
      <p class="glossary-notation-row">
        <code class="notation-display-tokens glossary-inline-notation"><span class="notation-token notation-set">pixie</span> + <span class="notation-token notation-modifier">ducking</span> + <span class="notation-token notation-core-family">butterfly</span></code>
        <span class="glossary-notation-shorthand">&rarr; <strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/phoenix">PHOENIX</a></strong></span>
      </p>
      <p class="glossary-notation-row">
        <code class="notation-display-tokens glossary-inline-notation"><span class="notation-token notation-modifier">gyro</span> + <span class="notation-token notation-core-family">torque</span></code>
        <span class="glossary-notation-shorthand">&rarr; <strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/mobius">MOBIUS</a></strong></span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>A beginner example: <code class="notation-display-tokens glossary-inline-notation">{{#with content.notationExamples.whirl}}{{#each tokens}}<span class="notation-token notation-{{cssRole}}" data-role="{{role}}" title="{{label}}">{{text}}</span>{{#unless @last}} {{/unless}}{{/each}}{{/with}}</code></h3>
    <p>
      A trick whose notation is just <code class="notation-display-tokens glossary-inline-notation">{{#with content.notationExamples.whirl}}{{#each tokens}}<span class="notation-token notation-{{cssRole}}" data-role="{{role}}" title="{{label}}">{{text}}</span>{{/each}}{{/with}}</code>
      is itself the family anchor: one inward dex and a cross-body clipper
      catch, with no modifiers stacked on and no set primitive prefacing it.
      A single token tells you: this is the structural root.
    </p>

    <h3>A compound example: <code class="notation-display-tokens glossary-inline-notation">{{#with content.notationExamples.paradoxWhirl}}{{#each tokens}}<span class="notation-token notation-{{cssRole}}" data-role="{{role}}" title="{{label}}">{{text}}</span>{{#unless @last}} {{/unless}}{{/each}}{{/with}}</code></h3>
    <p>
      The paradox modifier on a whirl base. The modifier (yellow-olive)
      shapes the body position; the base anchor (green) carries the dex, the
      clipper ending and the family identity. +1 ADD on top of whirl.
    </p>

    <h3>A modifier-heavy example: <code class="notation-display-tokens glossary-inline-notation">{{#with content.notationExamples.gauntlet}}{{#each tokens}}<span class="notation-token notation-{{cssRole}}" data-role="{{role}}" title="{{label}}">{{text}}</span>{{#unless @last}} {{/unless}}{{/each}}{{/with}}</code></h3>
    <p>
      Three operators (the stepping set, a duck and paradox) stacked on a
      torque base; the community calls it Gauntlet, 7 ADD. Each contributes
      its own +1 above the base.
    </p>

    <h3 id="notation-roles">Color-coded structural roles</h3>
    <p>
      The visual hierarchy is restrained: only the four primary roles
      (core family, set, rotation, body modifier) carry color saturation.
      Hover any token on a trick page to see its role and resolved family name.
    </p>

    <h3 id="abbreviations">Abbreviations</h3>
    <p>Shorthand that appears in trick titles and composition prose. The bracketed tokens inside operational notation are listed under <a href="#operational-notation">Operational notation</a> below.</p>
    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Trick-name shorthand">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Abbreviation</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {{#each content.abbreviations.trickNames}}
          <tr><td><code>{{short}}</code></td><td>{{meaning}}</td></tr>
          {{/each}}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    {{! ── Operational notation: advanced subsection ───────────────────────
        Reference for the bracket-flag operational notation token vocabulary.
        Collapsed by default so §7 reads primer-first; direct
        navigation to #operational-notation or any #op-* child anchor
        auto-opens the details element. }}
    <details class="glossary-operational-details" id="operational-notation">
      <summary class="glossary-operational-details-summary">
        <span class="glossary-operational-details-title">Operational notation</span>
        <span class="glossary-operational-details-hint">Bracket-flag token vocabulary: click to expand</span>
      </summary>

    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Operational notation">
    <table class="data-table glossary-layer-contrast">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Describes</th><th>Renders as</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Semantic</strong></td>
          <td>Compositional structure: what the trick <em>is</em></td>
          <td>Operator stack (e.g., <code>stepping ducking paradox torque</code>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Operational</strong></td>
          <td>Execution mechanics: what the body <em>does</em></td>
          <td>Bracket-flagged sequence (e.g., <code>CLIP &gt; OP IN [DEX] &gt; OP OUT [DEX] &gt; [DEL] CLIP</code>)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The two layers are complementary, not interchangeable. The same trick
      may be named at several semantic stopping depths yet still have a
      single canonical operational form. Trick pages show both when both are
      authored, the operational form under &ldquo;Execution notation&rdquo;;
      the reference below is its token vocabulary.
    </p>

    <h4>Component flags</h4>
    <p>Square-bracket annotations on a move that mark which structural component is in play. Multiple flags may stack on a single move.</p>
    <dl class="operational-glossary">
      <dt id="op-flag-dex"><code>[DEX]</code></dt>
      <dd>Dexterity component: the move involves bag-foot interaction (the foot circles around or contacts the bag).</dd>
      <dt id="op-flag-del"><code>[DEL]</code></dt>
      <dd>Stall component, written <code>[DEL]</code> from delay, the older word for a stall: the move ends in a stall on a foot or surface. Typically the final segment of an operational notation string.</dd>
      <dt id="op-flag-bod"><code>[BOD]</code></dt>
      <dd>Body-position component: the move includes a body pose change (spin, duck, dive, or a no-plant body event).</dd>
      <dt id="op-flag-xbd"><code>[XBD]</code></dt>
      <dd>Cross-body component: <code>[XBD]</code> records a cross-body configuration or traversal across the body's centreline. It is independent of the component's SAME/OP relation; both SAME and OP components can be cross-body. Typically appears with <code>[DEL]</code> on the landing.</dd>
      <dt id="op-flag-pdx"><code>[PDX]</code></dt>
      <dd>Paradox component: marks the paradox relationship on a dexterity. It is independent of <code>[XBD]</code> and is not an IN/OUT direction. <code>CLIP &gt; OP IN [DEX]</code> is a common entry example, not the definition; a later mid-trick dexterity can also carry <code>[PDX]</code>. Equivalent to the <code>paradox</code> dex relationship in semantic notation.</dd>
      <dt id="op-flag-xdex"><code>[XDEX]</code></dt>
      <dd>X-Dex component. Marks the conditional +1 a dex scores when the notation carries <code>[XDEX]</code> (see <a href="#term-x-dex">X-Dex</a>). Recorded only where the notation carries it; some external references use the flag more broadly.</dd>
      <dt id="op-flag-uns"><code>[UNS]</code></dt>
      <dd>Unusual-surface component: the stall lands on a non-standard surface (sole, knee, head, neck, shoulder, forehead, cloud) rather than a toe or clipper. Scores like any stall; the flag records that the landing surface is unusual.</dd>
      <dt id="op-flag-kick"><code>[KICK]</code></dt>
      <dd>Kick action marker: the move ends in a kick rather than a stall. Non-scoring: a trick that ends <code>[KICK]</code> rather than <code>[DEL]</code> adds no component for the kick.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h4>Sides</h4>
    <p>The side prefix on a move is relative to the previously referenced leg (the
      most recent side-bearing component), not to a fixed plant foot: <code>SAME</code>
      and <code>OP</code> say which leg acts compared with the leg that component
      used. A component that names no leg does not reset the reference. Because each
      side reads against the last one, the relationship chains, and two <code>OP</code>
      steps in a row come back to the original leg.</p>
    <dl class="operational-glossary">
      <dt id="op-side-same"><code>SAME</code></dt>
      <dd>The same leg as the most recent side-bearing component. Roughly equivalent to the <code>SS</code> abbreviation seen in some trick names.</dd>
      <dt id="op-side-op"><code>OP</code></dt>
      <dd>The opposite leg from the most recent side-bearing component. The other leg performs the move.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p id="op-side-worked-example">Worked example, tracking which leg acts as you read left to right:</p>
    <pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="op-side-worked-example"><code class="font-mono">LEFT set &gt; OP dex   = RIGHT dex
RIGHT dex &gt; OP catch = LEFT catch</code></pre>
    <p>So a set and a catch that are each <code>OP</code> relative to the step before
      them land back on the setting leg, not on the opposite one.</p>

    <h4>Directions</h4>
    <p>The arc-direction tokens describe the bag's path during the dex.</p>
    <dl class="operational-glossary">
      <dt id="op-direction-in"><code>IN</code></dt>
      <dd>Inward arc: the bag travels toward the body.</dd>
      <dt id="op-direction-out"><code>OUT</code></dt>
      <dd>Outward arc: the bag travels away from the body.</dd>
      <dt id="op-direction-front"><code>FRONT</code> / <code>BACK</code></dt>
      <dd>
        Forward / backward direction. Standalone <code>FRONT</code> or
        <code>BACK</code> describes the arc; when followed by
        <code>WHIRL</code> or <code>SWIRL</code>, the two tokens fuse
        into a single rotational-variant move (e.g.
        <code>FRONT WHIRL</code>, <code>FRONT SWIRL</code>).
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h4 id="op-sequence-operators">Sequence operators</h4>
    <p>The arrow tokens segment the operational notation into discrete sub-actions.</p>
    <dl class="operational-glossary">
      <dt id="op-seq-minor"><code>&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd>Sub-step boundary: continuous flow within a beat group. The next move follows directly from the previous one without major pause or replant.</dd>
      <dt id="op-seq-major"><code>&gt;&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd>Major step boundary: often signals a no-plant break, a beat change, or a body-action transition. Visually heavier than <code>&gt;</code>; typically pairs with <code>(no plant while)</code> on a following move.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h4 id="op-pre-states">Pre-move state flags</h4>
    <p>Lowercase parenthesized annotations that describe the body state during the next move.</p>
    <dl class="operational-glossary">
      <dt id="op-prestate-back"><code>(back)</code> / <code>(front)</code></dt>
      <dd>Backward / forward direction of the next body action. Most often seen with <code>SPIN</code>: <code>(back) SPIN [BOD]</code> = backward body spin.</dd>
      <dt id="op-prestate-no-plant"><code>(no plant while)</code></dt>
      <dd>The support leg does not plant during this segment. Common in dense compounds where the body is mid-air during the move.</dd>
      <dt id="op-prestate-rooted"><code>(rooted)</code></dt>
      <dd>Held / rooted position; no plant. Corresponds to the <code>rooted</code> set primitive (see the <a href="/freestyle/sets#historical-move-set-reference">Historical Move-Set Reference</a>).</dd>
    </dl>

    <h4 id="op-surfaces">Surfaces &amp; body actions (reference)</h4>
    <p>The remaining vocabulary is small enough to list verbatim:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>CLIP</code>, <code>TOE</code>: standard plant or landing surfaces; <code>INSIDE</code>, <code>OUTSIDE</code>, <code>SOLE</code>, <code>KNEE</code>, <code>HEEL</code>, <code>HEAD</code>, <code>NECK</code>, <code>SHOULDER</code>, <code>FOREHEAD</code>, <code>CLOUD</code>: unusual landing surfaces (flagged <code>[UNS]</code>)</li>
      <li><code>SPIN</code>, <code>DUCK</code>, <code>DIVE</code>, <code>JUMP</code>, <code>FLYING</code>: body actions</li>
      <li><code>SWING</code>: a controlled swing element (Pendulum, Rake); <code>SYMP</code>: the symposium dex movement</li>
      <li><code>KICK</code>: a terminating kick, non-scoring (flagged <code>[KICK]</code>)</li>
      <li><code>FRONT WHIRL</code> / <code>BACK WHIRL</code> / <code>FRONT SWIRL</code>: rotational dex variants on a rotational base (the plain swirl and reverse-swirl dexes are written with the ordinary IN / OUT vocabulary)</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="detail-muted">
      For the broader set notation reference (set primitives like Pixie,
      Fairy, Atomic, Quantum and how they decompose), see
      <a href="/freestyle/sets#historical-move-set-reference">Historical Move-Set Reference</a>.
    </p>
    </details>
  </section>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="dict-tile" id="chapter-add-accounting">
    <summary class="dict-tile-summary">
      <h2 class="dict-tile-title" id="traditional-reference">ADD Accounting</h2>
      <span class="dict-tile-hint">Where a trick's difficulty number comes from, and how the weights add up.</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="dict-tile-body">
    <section class="content-section" id="section-add-accounting">

    <h3 id="run-quality">ADD philosophy</h3>
    <p>
      ADD is <em>additive structural accounting</em>: a trick's total is the
      sum of its base weight and each modifier's weight. The scored
      bracket-flags in operational notation are
      <code>[DEX]</code> / <code>[BOD]</code> / <code>[XBD]</code> /
      <code>[PDX]</code> / <code>[DEL]</code> / <code>[UNS]</code> /
      <code>[XDEX]</code> (the last scored only where the notation carries it);
      <code>[KICK]</code> marks a non-scoring kick. The same additive logic lets
      the dictionary read <em><a href="/freestyle/tricks/mobius">mobius</a></em>
      as &ldquo;gyro torque&rdquo; and arrive at the same total either way.
    </p>

    {{!-- The 9-ADD wording is deliberately anonymized: it names no witness and
          claims no record. Nine-ADD landings are reported but unverified and
          disputed, so the highest video-corroborated ceiling is stated at 8. --}}
    <p class="glossary-note" id="difficulty-frontier">
      <strong>The highest ADD.</strong> Eight ADD is the highest landing in the
      dictionary that video confirms. Carousel (Surging Ducking Paradox
      Symposium Whirling Rake) is the filmed example, and Surging Ducking
      Paradox Torque is a close 8-ADD contender. Nine-ADD landings have
      been reported but never confirmed on video.
    </p>

    <h3 id="add-provenance">Where do ADD values come from?</h3>
    <p>
      A trick's ADD can come from three places, and they do not always match.
      The <strong>source-stated</strong> value is the number an external
      catalogue recorded. The <strong>derived</strong> value is what the platform
      computes from the trick's structure, summing each scoring element. The
      <strong>structural-derivation</strong> value is the sum of a named
      compound's parts (eggbeater = atomic + legover + stall = 3). When the
      stated and derived numbers disagree, the derived value is canonical and the
      source number is recorded as a divergence.
    </p>
    <p>
      This happens for two reasons. Sometimes a source counts a different thing:
      PassBack records dexes, while the platform counts ADD, so a mirage is one
      dex but two ADD (dex plus stall) and a butterfly is one dex but three ADD.
      Neither is wrong; they measure different things. Other times a catalogue
      carries a convention that diverges from structure, as with Big Apple Sauce
      and the furious and railing families (see <a href="#section-sources">Sources</a>).
      Ontology can override a catalogue number because the catalogue records what
      was observed, while the structural ADD states what the move is made of.
    </p>

    <h3>Base trick weights</h3>
    <p>
      The twelve canonical atoms each have a fixed ADD value; modifier
      stacks compose onto them.
    </p>
    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Base trick weights">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Atom</th><th>ADD</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>toe-stall</td><td>1</td><td>foundational stall</td></tr>
          <tr><td>clipper-stall</td><td>2</td><td>cross-body traversal + stall</td></tr>
          <tr><td>around-the-world</td><td>2</td><td>1-dex atom</td></tr>
          <tr><td>orbit</td><td>2</td><td>reverse around-the-world</td></tr>
          <tr><td>legover</td><td>2</td><td>1-dex toe terminal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>pickup</td><td>2</td><td>reverse legover</td></tr>
          <tr><td>mirage</td><td>2</td><td>dex-anchored entry topology</td></tr>
          <tr><td>illusion</td><td>2</td><td>reverse mirage</td></tr>
          <tr><td>butterfly</td><td>3</td><td>dex+xbody+stall</td></tr>
          <tr><td>osis</td><td>3</td><td>body+xbody+stall</td></tr>
          <tr><td>whirl</td><td>3</td><td>dex+xbody+stall</td></tr>
          <tr><td>swirl</td><td>3</td><td>swirl+xbody+stall</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Modifier weights</h3>
    <p>
      Most modifiers add a fixed ADD weight on every base; an
      <a href="#term-x-dex">X-Dex</a> is never part of a modifier's own weight.
      Blurry is the exception: it is a folk shorthand scored per trick, reading
      as stepping with a paradox second dex (+2) where the trick's notation
      carries that second dex and as stepping alone (+1) where it does not, so
      its contribution follows the trick's own notation rather than a single
      global value.
    </p>
    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Modifier weights">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Modifier</th><th>ADD</th><th>Class</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>paradox</td><td>+1</td><td>dex relationship</td></tr>
          <tr><td>stepping</td><td>+1</td><td>set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ducking</td><td>+1</td><td>body</td></tr>
          <tr><td>weaving</td><td>+1</td><td>ducking set (the duck carries the +1)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>diving</td><td>+1</td><td>body</td></tr>
          <tr><td>zulu</td><td>+1</td><td>ducking set (the duck carries the +1)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>symposium</td><td>+1</td><td>body (no-plant)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>spinning</td><td>+1</td><td>body (rotation)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gyro</td><td>+1</td><td>body (rotation)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>inspinning</td><td>+1</td><td>body (rotation)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>whirling</td><td>+1</td><td>body (rotation)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>atomic</td><td>+1</td><td>set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>furious</td><td>+2</td><td>two-dex set (barraging is a legacy name for this same set, not a separate operator)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>nuclear</td><td>+2</td><td>set (composite ≡ paradox+illusion)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>blurry</td><td>+1 / +2</td><td>set (folk shorthand, scored per trick)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>pixie</td><td>+1</td><td>set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>fairy</td><td>+1</td><td>set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>quantum</td><td>+1</td><td>set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>pogo</td><td>+1</td><td>set (the standalone pogo set scores 0)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>shooting</td><td>+3</td><td>set (outlier magnitude)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>rooted</td><td>0</td><td>set (non-scoring)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Additive formulas</h3>
    <p>
      ADD = base + modifier stack: <code>paradox(+1) + mirage(2) = 3 ADD</code>,
      and the operational notation chain mirrors the formula's term order.
      Multi-modifier compounds walk the chain outward from the base:
      <em>stepping paradox whirl</em> =
      <code>stepping(+1) + paradox(+1) + whirl(3) = 5 ADD</code>, the same chain
      that reads as the trick's composition on its detail page. Folk labels for
      two-operator stacks are decomposed into their operators so the arithmetic
      surfaces: <code>blurry = stepping + paradox</code> (see
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks/blur">Blur</a> below).
    </p>

    <h3>Worked examples</h3>
    <p>
      Each card shows the compact reading, the official ADD, and the
      derivation; open &ldquo;Why it matters&rdquo; for the commentary.
    </p>
    <div class="glossary-add-examples-grid">
      {{#each content.addWorkedExamples}}
      <article class="glossary-add-example-card" id="add-example-{{slug}}">
        <header class="glossary-add-example-head">
          <h4 class="glossary-add-example-title">{{displayName}}</h4>
          <span class="glossary-add-example-status glossary-add-example-status--{{status}}">{{statusLabel}}</span>
        </header>
        <dl class="glossary-add-example-fields">
          <dt>Compact</dt>
          <dd>{{compactNotation}}</dd>
          <dt>Official ADD</dt>
          <dd class="glossary-add-example-add">{{officialAdd}}</dd>
          <dt>Derivation</dt>
          <dd><code>{{derivation}}</code></dd>
        </dl>
        {{#if observationalNote}}
        <details class="glossary-family-card-observational">
          <summary class="glossary-family-card-observational-summary">
            <span class="glossary-family-card-observational-title">Why it matters</span>
            <span class="glossary-layer-badge glossary-layer-badge--observational">observational</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="glossary-family-card-observational-body">{{observationalNote}}</p>
        </details>
        {{/if}}
      </article>
      {{/each}}
    </div>

    <details class="glossary-family-card-observational">
      <summary class="glossary-family-card-observational-summary">
        <span class="glossary-family-card-observational-title">Discrepancy cases &amp; policy-dependent semantics</span>
        <span class="glossary-layer-badge glossary-layer-badge--advanced">advanced</span>
      </summary>
      <p class="glossary-family-card-observational-body">
        Some compounds show a gap between the naïvely computed ADD and the
        official value (for example sumo, via an X-Dex on the following dex).
        These are settled cases where the mechanism is known, and trick-detail
        pages render the official value with the divergence noted. Where outside
        sources disagree with the official value, the usual reasons are side
        cues they count as additive, a different decomposition depth, or an
        older convention since retired. One question stays open: whether certain
        tricks, chiefly the blurry-named ones, carry a paradox term at all, since
        some sources write a <code>[PDX]</code> bracket where others omit it.
        Where the bracket is written it scores like any other. See
        <a href="/freestyle/add-analysis">ADD Accounting &amp; Analysis</a> for
        each case.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>
        <span class="glossary-layer-badge glossary-layer-badge--advanced">[advanced]</span>
        Multi-bag governing rule (boundary doctrine)
      </summary>
      <p>
        Multi-bag freestyle (controlling more than one bag at a time) is a
        boundary object relative to the single-bag freestyle dictionary that
        the rest of these chapters describe. The platform's governing rule
        for multi-bag ADD accounting is: <strong>one additional ADD for each
        simultaneously controlled bag</strong>. Two-bag juggling reads as
        <code>TOE &gt; TOE</code>, two simultaneous stalls scoring 2 ADD;
        three-bag juggling reads as <code>TOE &gt; TOE &gt; TOE</code>, 3 ADD;
        and so on. The number counts bags held at once, not the point value of
        one stall, which is why it is not written in the <code>stall(N)</code>
        form used for atomic scoring.
      </p>
      <p>
        Multi-bag tricks form a category of their own, which the single-bag
        dictionary views (ADD, family, set, modifier) do not classify.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>
        <span class="glossary-layer-badge glossary-layer-badge--advanced">[advanced]</span>
        ADD value can flatten execution difficulty
      </summary>
      <p>
        Some historically assigned ADD values flatten major differences in
        execution difficulty or athletic demand. The clearest example sits at
        the 2 ADD layer: hop-over, walk-over, and wrap are all historically
        classified as 2-ADD moves despite substantial differences in balance,
        timing, mobility, and aerial coordination. Hop-over carries a body
        jump over a held stall (mid-air clearance, single landing leg);
        walk-over passes the opposite leg over a grounded stall (balance, not
        jump); wrap pulls the bag around the support leg into a cross-body
        position (no body event at all). The three share the held-stall
        leg-over chassis but otherwise have very different physical demands.
      </p>
      <p>
        The platform preserves historical ADD doctrine (the published value
        stays canonical) while allowing structural and mechanical relationships
        to be discussed separately on the per-trick detail pages and via the
        family and modifier browse views. The same pattern
        recurs at other ADD layers (1-ADD body kicks vs anatomical stalls;
        3-ADD compounds whose execution difficulty diverges from their flat
        ADD-arithmetic). ADD is one axis of difficulty, not all of it.
      </p>
    </details>

    <p>
      For the full mechanical walkthrough with all resolved formulas,
      see <a href="/freestyle/add-analysis">ADD Accounting &amp; Analysis</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 class="section-heading" id="run-quality-ladder">Run-quality ladder</h4>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      A run-quality tier describes a whole run, not a single trick. Each tier
      asks every trick in the run to clear a minimum ADD, so the
      <strong>lowest-ADD trick in the run sets the tier</strong>: one easy trick
      caps the whole run.
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      {{#each content.runQualityLadder}}
      <dt>{{term}}</dt>
      <dd>{{definition}}</dd>
      {{/each}}
    </dl>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      Example: a run of ADD 5, 3, and 6 tricks is <strong>Guiltless</strong>
      (every trick reaches at least 3), not Fearless, because the ADD-3 trick is
      the floor.
    </p>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      <strong>Genuine</strong> and <strong>BOP</strong> are related terms, not
      rungs of this numeric ladder: Genuine is Guiltless excluding BOP tricks,
      and BOP is the Butterfly, Osis, and Paradox Mirage set. For applying these
      tiers and the competitive formats (Sick3, Shred:30, Circle) to real runs,
      see <a href="/freestyle/combo-analysis">Combo Analysis</a>.
    </p>
    </section>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="dict-tile" id="chapter-runs-sequences">
    <summary class="dict-tile-summary">
      <h2 class="dict-tile-title">Runs &amp; Sequences</h2>
      <span class="dict-tile-hint">How individual tricks combine into runs.</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="dict-tile-body">
  <section class="content-section" id="section-composition">
    <p>
      Above the single-trick level, tricks combine into longer sequences. The
      vocabulary below describes how tricks are <em>used</em>, not how they
      are defined.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Run</strong>: a continuous sequence of tricks.</li>
      <li><strong>Shuffle</strong>: alternating sides rhythmically.</li>
      <li><strong>Link / Transition</strong>: the connection between tricks.</li>
      <li><strong>Connector Trick</strong>: a trick commonly used to maintain flow. Whirl-family tricks often act as connectors.</li>
      <li><strong>Dropless</strong>: a run without drops.</li>
      <li><strong>Density</strong>: average ADD per trick in a run.</li>
      <li><strong>Shred Circle</strong>: a group format where players take turns performing runs.</li>
    </ul>


    {{! ── Decomposition table — the key structural readings in one compact
        place. Sits this late in the page because it belongs after the reader
        knows families, modifiers, notation, and ADD. The `derivation-atlas`
        anchor is preserved for inbound deep-links. }}
    <h3 class="section-heading glossary-derivation-atlas-heading" id="derivation-atlas">
      Decomposition table
    </h3>
    <p class="glossary-derivation-atlas-intro">
      The most useful structural readings in one place. Each row reads a named
      trick as the operators and base it is built from. A <strong>&equiv;</strong>
      row is a structural equivalence; a <strong>&asymp;</strong> row is an
      educational approximation that communicates movement feel without replacing
      the formal classification.
    </p>
    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="derivation-atlas">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Trick</th><th>Reads as</th><th>Register</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/torque">Torque</a></td><td>Quantum Osis</td><td>&equiv;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/blender">Blender</a></td><td>Whirling Osis</td><td>&equiv;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/mobius">Mobius</a></td><td>Gyro Torque &equiv; Gyro Quantum Osis</td><td>&equiv;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/ripwalk">Ripwalk</a></td><td>Stepping Butterfly</td><td>&equiv;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/blur">Blur</a></td><td>Stepping Paradox Mirage</td><td>&equiv;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/twirl">Twirl</a></td><td>Reverse Swirling Osis <span class="detail-muted">&asymp; Swirl + Spin (movement feel)</span></td><td>&equiv; / &asymp;</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    {{! ── Vocabulary relationships — four ways trick names can relate ───────
        Replaces the prior #compression-vs-alternate-derivation subsection
        and the standalone #symbolic-compression-flow mobius example, which
        fold into the new structure. Both anchors preserved (on the h3 and
        on the Structural compression h4) for inbound deep-links from
        external references.

        Curator-authored: four relationship types
        (pure alias / structural compression / equivalent derivation /
        ontology relationship) explained with concrete movement-centered
        examples. Movement-language pedagogy, not academic ontology;
        avoids parser/AST terminology. Worked examples (flurry +
        witchdoctor) fold into Equivalent derivations subsection. }}
    <h3 class="section-heading" id="vocabulary-relationships">Vocabulary relationships: four ways trick names can relate</h3>
    {{! Preserve legacy anchor for deep-links; the prior subsection lived here. }}
    <a id="compression-vs-alternate-derivation"></a>
    <p>
      Freestyle names don&rsquo;t behave like ordinary dictionary words. One
      trick can have several names, and one name can stand for a longer
      movement formula or be reached by more than one route. A
      <strong>&equiv;</strong> reading on a trick page is
      <em>descriptive, never a recipe</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The boundary older vocabularies lost: <strong>a historical derivation is
      not a productive modifier.</strong>
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks/eggbeater">eggbeater</a> &equiv; atomic legover
      <span class="glossary-flagship-marker" title="Flagship: a historical reading is descriptive, not a productive operator">&#9733;</span>
      is two vocabularies describing one movement; it does <em>not</em>
      make &ldquo;eggbeater&rdquo; an operator you can apply (there is no
      &ldquo;eggbeater-ing&rdquo; another base). A genuine
      <a href="#section-modifiers">modifier ecosystem</a> (pixie, ducking) is
      the opposite: it <em>does</em> apply across many bases. A reading says
      what a trick <em>is equivalent to</em>; a modifier says what you can
      <em>do to</em> a base.
    </p>
    <p>
      A reading can also name a structure built from other tricks:
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks/blender">blender</a> &equiv; whirling osis.
      Some readings are settled; others are still open and may change. Four
      kinds of relationship come up often enough to name separately.
    </p>

    <h4 class="glossary-equivalence-worked-heading">1. Pure aliases: different names, same trick</h4>
    <p>
      The simplest case: one operational structure carries several names,
      usually for historical reasons. Before <em>quantum</em> entered the
      vocabulary, players said <em>toe</em> for the same compounds; that older
      naming is retired (see <a href="#section-historical">Historical Terms</a>).
    </p>
    <ul class="glossary-equivalence-worked-example">
      <li><strong>toe blur</strong> &equiv; <a href="/freestyle/tricks/quantum_mirage">quantum mirage</a></li>
      <li><strong>toe blizzard</strong> &equiv; <a href="/freestyle/tricks/quantum_illusion">quantum illusion</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Aliases don&rsquo;t expose movement structure: they are naming, not
      composition, for one physical sequence.
    </p>

    {{! Preserve legacy anchor for deep-links to the mobius compression flow. }}
    <h4 class="glossary-equivalence-worked-heading" id="symbolic-compression-flow">2. Structural compression: names that encode composition</h4>
    <p>
      A trick name can compress a longer compositional reading into a single
      word. Many commonly repeated compositions acquired compressed names that
      became more familiar than their expansions: the short name is the
      everyday speech form, and the expansion reveals how the trick is built.
    </p>
    <ul class="glossary-equivalence-worked-example">
      <li><strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/smear">smear</a></strong> &equiv; pixie mirage</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/ripwalk">ripwalk</a></strong> <span class="glossary-flagship-marker" title="Flagship teaching example: clean atom-level structural compression">&#9733;</span> &equiv; stepping butterfly</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/atom_smasher">atom smasher</a></strong> &equiv; atomic mirage</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/eggbeater">eggbeater</a></strong> &equiv; atomic legover</li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/mobius">mobius</a></strong> <span class="glossary-flagship-marker" title="Flagship teaching example: multi-depth compression ladder">&#9733;</span>
        &equiv; gyro torque
        &equiv; spinning same-side torque
        &equiv; spinning quantum same-side osis
        <em>(a compression ladder: same structure at four reading depths)</em>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Unlike aliases, these names <em>are</em> structural. Reading
      <em>ripwalk</em> as <em>stepping butterfly</em> makes the move&rsquo;s
      ancestry legible. A compressed name can sit at several expansion depths,
      as the mobius ladder shows. For how operators stack and ADD totals are
      derived, see
      <a href="/freestyle/add-analysis">ADD Accounting &amp; Analysis</a>.
    </p>


    <h4 class="glossary-equivalence-worked-heading">3. Equivalent derivations: multiple valid paths</h4>
    <p>
      Some tricks admit more than one structural reading: different operator
      compositions that arrive at the same trick with the same ADD. Neither path
      is wrong.
    </p>
    <ul class="glossary-equivalence-worked-example">
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/flurry">flurry</a></strong> <span class="glossary-flagship-marker" title="Flagship teaching example: two valid derivations of the same trick">&#9733;</span>
        &equiv; <em>furious legover</em>
        <span>(furious(+2) + legover(2) = 4 ADD)</span>
        &equiv; <em>paradox + paradox legover</em>
        <span>(paradox(+1) + paradox-legover(3) = 4 ADD)</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/witchdoctor">witchdoctor</a></strong>
        &equiv; <em><a href="/freestyle/tricks/atom_smasher">atom-smasher</a> + symposium</em>
        <span>(atom-smasher(4) + symposium(+1) = 5 ADD; current canonical)</span>
        &equiv; <em>atomic symposium mirage</em>
        <span class="text-muted">(historical reading, preserved as context)</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A trick page&rsquo;s &ldquo;Alternate derivations&rdquo; panel shows
      exactly this: structurally distinct routes that converge on the same
      trick.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some equivalent derivations survive primarily as
      <strong>historical readings</strong>: the language a trick was originally
      taught in, kept after a different canonical decomposition became
      preferred. Witchdoctor&rsquo;s <em>atomic symposium mirage</em> reading,
      the retired <em>toe</em>-prefixed naming, and several barrage-family
      decompositions sit here. They are kept so older community language stays
      navigable, not as competing claims.
    </p>

    <h4 class="glossary-equivalence-worked-heading">4. Ontology relationships: family ancestry and transformations</h4>
    <p>
      Tricks also relate by ancestry: a base anchors a family of compounds, and
      modifiers transform one trick into another along well-worn paths.
    </p>
    <ul class="glossary-equivalence-worked-example">
      <li>The <strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/butterfly">butterfly family</a></strong> anchors a walking progression (butterfly &rarr; ducking butterfly &rarr; spinning butterfly &rarr; &hellip;) where each step swaps or adds one operator.</li>
      <li>The <strong>legover family</strong> (<a href="/freestyle/tricks/legover">legover</a>, <a href="/freestyle/tricks/double_leg_over">double-leg-over</a>, <a href="/freestyle/tricks/eggbeater">eggbeater</a>, <a href="/freestyle/tricks/schmoe">schmoe</a>) share the same body event (leg over the bag) under different setup vehicles.</li>
      <li>The <strong>whirl family</strong> (<a href="/freestyle/tricks/whirl">whirl</a>, <a href="/freestyle/tricks/rev_whirl">rev-whirl</a>, <a href="/freestyle/tricks/gyro_whirl">gyro-whirl</a>, <a href="/freestyle/tricks/spinning_whirl">spinning-whirl</a>, <a href="/freestyle/tricks/paradox_whirl">paradox-whirl</a>) share the same rotational dex with different prefixes.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/eclipse">eclipse</a> &rarr; <a href="/freestyle/tricks/hop_over">hop-over</a></strong>: eclipse is the airborne evolution of hop-over&rsquo;s grounded held-stall leg-over chassis.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These are neither aliases (the names refer to different tricks) nor
      compression (each member is a distinct trick). They are transformational:
      one trick is reachable from another by adding a body event, a rotation,
      or lifting it airborne.
    </p>

    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Ontology relationships: family ancestry and transformations">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Same trick?</th><th>Same structure?</th><th>What it asserts</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Pure alias</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>different name, identical sequence</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Structural compression</td><td>yes</td><td>yes (compressed)</td><td>the short name packages a compositional formula</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Equivalent derivation</td><td>yes</td><td>different structural reading/path (movement converges; derivation differs)</td><td>different operator paths arrive at the same trick &amp; ADD</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ontology relationship</td><td>no (distinct trick)</td><td>related</td><td>linked by family ancestry or structural transformation</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Advanced names can seem cryptic because the notation describes
      relationships between movement structures, not just labels. Knowing which
      kind of relationship you are looking at tells you how to read the name:
      vocabulary, composition, derivation, or family.
    </p>

    <h4 class="glossary-equivalence-worked-heading" id="two-projections">Two projections of one move, and which name becomes official</h4>
    <p>
      Underneath all four relationships sits one idea: a trick&rsquo;s
      <strong>folk name</strong> and its <strong>structural decomposition</strong>
      (the operators and base it is built from) are <strong>two projections of
      the same move</strong>, neither more &ldquo;real&rdquo; than the other.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is also how the platform decides two names mean the same trick: it
      compares the <strong>decomposed structure</strong>, not the spelling. Two
      names that look nothing alike are the same move when they decompose to the
      same operators and base.
    </p>
    <ul class="glossary-equivalence-worked-example">
      <li><strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/gauntlet">gauntlet</a></strong> &equiv; <em>stepping-ducking-paradox-torque</em>: a folk name and its structural reading, one 7-ADD move.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/freestyle/tricks/gyro_ducking_symposium_torque">gyro-ducking-symposium-torque</a></strong> &equiv; <em>alpine-big-apple</em>: a structural name and its folk reading, one 7-ADD move.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Which projection becomes the <strong>canonical</strong> (displayed) name
      depends on which one the community actually uses, and it goes both ways:
      for <em>gauntlet</em> the folk name won, for
      <em>gyro-ducking-symposium-torque</em> the structural name won. The other
      projection stays attached as an alias, so either name still finds the
      trick.
    </p>

