  <section class="content-section glossary-connective-section" id="inside-clipper-neighborhood">
    <h2 class="section-heading">
      Case study: the inside-stall stationary-transition neighborhood
      <span class="symbolic-layer-badge" title="Observational movement-neighborhood lens: supplementary; does not change canonical classifications">observational</span>
    </h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Wrap, walk-over / step-over, hop-over, and eclipse share a distinctive
      <strong>inside-stall stationary transition</strong>: the support-side body
      moves around a relatively stationary bag rather than sending the bag
      through space. They look nearly identical to a newer player, yet earn their
      difficulty in different ways, which is the lesson: <strong>ADD is not a pure
      difficulty metric, and embodied movement is its own axis</strong>.
    </p>
    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Case study: the inside-stall stationary-transition neighborhood">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Trick</th><th>Dominant mechanism</th><th>ADD logic</th><th>Observational feel</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/wrap">Wrap</a></td><td>dexterity</td><td>stall + dex</td><td>compact dex around a stationary bag</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/walk_over">Walk-over</a> / Step-over</td><td>dexterity</td><td>stall + dex</td><td>stepping body-over motion</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/hop_over">Hop-over</a></td><td>body</td><td>stall + body</td><td>full-body transition around the bag</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/freestyle/tricks/eclipse">Eclipse</a></td><td>extended hop-over topology</td><td>hop-over + additional commitment</td><td>symposium-like escalation of hop-over mechanics</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="glossary-note">
      <strong>Why it matters.</strong> Hop-over is widely felt as harder than the
      dex-based 2-ADD variants despite the identical ADD; the accounting that keeps
      all three at 2 ADD is under &ldquo;ADD value can flatten execution
      difficulty&rdquo; in <a href="#section-add-accounting">ADD Accounting</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="glossary-note">
      <strong>Eclipse.</strong> Eclipse adds a further commitment layer to the
      hop-over movement (commonly read as symposium), though its precise
      decomposition is <strong>unsettled</strong>; the notation and this movement
      reading coexist. See <a href="#tracking-vs-canonization">Tracking is not
      canonization</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="symbolic-layer-footer symbolic-layer-footer--glossary">
      Observational movement-neighborhood lens. Grouping is by player-felt movement envelope;
      the canonical family classifications (wrap, walk-over, hop-over, and eclipse each anchor
      their own family) are unchanged.
    </p>
  </section>

  {{! ── Advanced Reference Concepts — the publication-state vocabulary.
      Sits this late in the page because it is reference
      machinery, not onboarding; the `how-to-read` anchor is preserved for
      inbound deep-links. }}
  <section class="content-section" id="section-advanced-reference">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Advanced Reference Concepts</h2>
    <p>
      These distinctions mark what kind of claim each entry makes. They matter
      for deep reference; a first read does not need them.
    </p>

    <h3 class="section-heading" id="primitives-and-compounds">Primitives and compounds</h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>foundational primitive</strong> (also called a
      <em>core atom</em> or simply an <em>atom</em>) is an irreducible
      movement, treated as a single unit rather than a composition of smaller
      parts: the twelve core atoms of
      <a href="#core-trick-atoms">Movement Basics</a>. Each carries an internal
      structural reading (xbody + dex + stall, for instance) that names the
      components of the motion, but there is no shallower trick it decomposes
      into.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>compound</strong> (also called a
      <em>compositional structure</em>) is a trick built by applying
      modifiers, operators, or composition rules to one or more atoms:
      paradox-mirage, atomsmasher, <a href="/freestyle/tricks/mobius">mobius</a>,
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks/blur">blur</a>, and baroque. Compounds read as
      decomposition chains and may have several equivalence paths, which
      trick pages show in an &ldquo;Alternate derivations&rdquo; panel.
    </p>
    <p>
      The distinction matters in two places:
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-primitives-compounds">
      <dt>Equivalence and compression analysis applies to compounds, not primitives.</dt>
      <dd>
        When a compound has more than one structurally-valid decomposition,
        the trick-detail page surfaces both readings as alternate
        derivations. Atoms intentionally omit this layer; they are
        the substrate the compositions are built from, not themselves
        further compressible.
      </dd>
      <dt>Decomposition is a structural claim, not a difficulty claim.</dt>
      <dd>
        Primitive vs compound is about ontology, not difficulty or scoring.
        Some atoms are mechanically demanding; some compounds are easier
        than the atoms they are built on. ADD values are independent of
        the distinction: atoms span 1&ndash;3 ADD, compounds span 2
        to 8 ADD, and primitive-vs-compound tracks movement structure,
        not scored complexity.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      The same primitive/compound distinction applies to modifiers and
      operators. Stepping, paradox, atomic, and the other named modifiers
      are primitive operators; blurry (= stepping paradox) and the other
      operator-compositions are compound operators. The doctrine is the
      same in either case: primitives compose, compounds decompose.
    </p>

    <h3 class="section-heading" id="tracking-vs-canonization">Tracking is not canonization</h3>
    <p>
      Not every name in the dictionary is at the same stage. Here is what each
      label means, what you see, and why it is there:
    </p>
    <div class="records-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="tracking-vs-canonization">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>State</th><th>In plain words</th><th>What you see</th><th>Why it is here</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Canonical</td><td>A settled trick the community agrees on</td><td>Normal name, no badge</td><td>Its name and its structure are both resolved</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tracked</td><td>A name people really use, written down but not yet ruled on</td><td>Listed, flagged not-yet-canonical</td><td>A complete record kept before the case is settled (for example &ldquo;Big Applesauce&rdquo;, &ldquo;Alpine Big Apple&rdquo;)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Under review</td><td>A real trick whose breakdown is not confirmed</td><td>A &ldquo;decomposition under review&rdquo; pill, or an <em>advanced</em> badge</td><td>The move exists; its structure is still open</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Observational</td><td>A &ldquo;feels-similar&rdquo; grouping or alternate reading, offered as a hint</td><td>An <em>observational</em> badge</td><td>It helps you see connections; it is not an official ruling</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Alias / duplicate</td><td>A second name for a trick that already has one</td><td>Resolves to the main trick</td><td>Two names that decompose to the same move; one is displayed, the other kept so either still finds it</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A name becomes a published trick only once its structure is settled,
      not just its ADD.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="section-media-claim-scope">
    <h2 class="section-heading">
      What a video can and can&rsquo;t tell you
    </h2>
    <p>
      A video shows how a move looks and feels. It does not decide the
      move&rsquo;s structure, ADD, or family; those come from the
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks">trick dictionary</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each clip&rsquo;s badge says what kind of clip it is:
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-publication-states">
      <dt>Tutorial</dt>
      <dd>Walks through how to perform the trick: the strongest teaching claim a clip can make.</dd>
      <dt>Demonstration</dt>
      <dd>Shows the trick performed cleanly without a teaching breakdown: proof of the shape, not a lesson.</dd>
      <dt>Record</dt>
      <dd>A record-attempt clip with countable repetitions. Evidence of an achievement, not instruction; it never stands in for a tutorial.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      A tutorial can teach a move whose breakdown the dictionary still lists as
      open. The clip shows the movement; it does not settle the question.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each trick page shows its own clips, and broader collections are in the
      <a href="/media">media galleries</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

