  <section class="content-section" id="section-sources">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Sources</h2>
    <p class="glossary-about-framing">
      This reference records vocabulary the footbag community built informally over four decades. Authoritative trick-level definitions live in the <a href="/freestyle/tricks">Trick Dictionary</a>; the source families below contribute to the language documented here.
    </p>
    <ul class="glossary-sources-list">
      <li><strong>Footbag.org</strong>: the long-running community archive; trick lists, event records, and the foundational vocabulary catalogued there. See also the historical per-trick move list.</li>
      <li><strong>PassBack</strong>: conceptual tutorial series which introduced and formalised much of the modern compositional vocabulary (set / modifier / body operator framings).</li>
      <li><strong>Stanford shorthand</strong>: compact symbolic notation, an alternative encoding of the compositional grammar that exposes structural symmetries the spelled-out names sometimes obscure.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.footbagmoves.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FootbagMoves</a></strong>: observational catalogue of named tricks, alternate names, and rotational variants; informs the symbolic-grammar layer of this reference.</li>
      <li><strong>AnzTrikz</strong>: Australian / New Zealand demonstration series; canonical references for many compound rotational variants.</li>
      <li><strong>Footbag Finland</strong>: Finnish freestyle demonstration footage; visual references for several dictionary entries.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="glossary-about-framing">
      <strong>Where authority comes from.</strong> These sources do not carry
      equal weight. When they conflict, the platform resolves in a fixed order:
      a curator ruling first; then the structural grammar, where a trick's
      difficulty is what its movement composition adds up to; then community
      usage, which decides the displayed name; and last an external catalogue's
      recorded number. An external source is strong evidence that a trick exists
      and is performed, but it does not override how a trick decomposes or what
      its structural ADD is.
    </p>
    <p class="glossary-about-framing">
      The vocabulary documented here rests on decades of foundational work
      by many people across the community, beginning with Ben Job's notation;
      their contributions to freestyle's notation, archives, and movement
      language have been indispensable to this reference and the dictionary it
      supports.
    </p>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      Definitions integrate the above sources without quoting any single
      one verbatim; where a term carries a contested decomposition, the
      authoritative trick page surfaces the open question explicitly.
    </p>
  </section>
